# Configuration for xfmcp. Copy to .env and set what you need.
#
# A commented-out setting shows the DEFAULT it takes when left unset, so a working install
# can set nothing here at all - the file that has to exist is the forum inventory.
#
# A BLANK value means there is no literal default to show; the comment above it says what
# happens when it is left unset. Nothing here is an example value.
#
# A default shown as `storage/...` is relative to the application directory. Copying it
# onto an enabled line does not reproduce the default: a relative value resolves against
# the working directory, and `app:validate` fails it. Set it absolutely or leave it alone.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE GOES. It is looked for beside the binary, then at XFMCP_ENV, then in the
# project directory, then at /etc/xfmcp/.env. XFMCP_ENV names the file itself and has to be
# set in the environment the MCP client starts the server with: a file cannot say where it
# is. `xfmcp app:config` prints the file that was actually read.

# The forum inventory: one table per forum, with its API key. forums.toml.example is the
# template. An installed binary MUST set this absolutely, because its storage path follows
# the working directory and an MCP client starts it from a directory of its own choosing.
#XFMCP_FORUMS_PATH=storage/forums.toml

# A second timezone to show each time in. Every time a forum reports is UTC, and each tool
# adds it as ISO 8601 in UTC; set this to a zone such as Australia/Sydney and each is shown
# in that zone as well, with its offset. Left at UTC nothing is added.
#APP_TIMEZONE=UTC

# Seconds a request to a forum may take, and seconds to wait for the connection to open.
#XENFORO_TIMEOUT=10
#XENFORO_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=5

# Storage path. No literal default: from a checkout it is the project's storage/, and in a
# compiled binary it is the current working directory. Only the default inventory path
# depends on it, so setting XFMCP_FORUMS_PATH makes this unnecessary.
#LARAVEL_STORAGE_PATH=
